## DARE::DecisionAlternativeRiskEndurant

A DARE is an OODA state change. It moves a problem from
Observe/Orient (uncertainty, alternatives, risk assessment) into
Decide/Act (committed decision with a persistent enduring artifact).

Every DARE document MUST have exactly these four sections:

### Decision — Perdurant (Process/Event)
What we are committing to DO. This is an action that unfolds over time
— implementation, migration, adoption. The decision is not the artifact;
the decision is the act of choosing and executing.

```toml
[decision]
what = "Adopt josh-proxy for vendor submodule management"
who = "b00t-core (operator approval required)"
when = "Q3 2026"
scope = ["vendor/ layer", "CI pipeline", "agent sandboxing"]
```

### Alternative — Abstract (Counterfactual)
What else we considered and why we rejected it. These are
counterfactuals — they never materialized but shaped the decision.
Each alternative names itself, states why it was viable, and states
the specific reason it was not chosen.

```toml
[[alternatives]]
name = "git-subrepo"
viable = "Merges externals into a single repo without submodules"
rejected_because = "Loses upstream history; manual resync burden"

[[alternatives]]
name = "Do nothing"
viable = "Zero migration cost"
rejected_because = "20 submodules, agent safety surface, CI pain compounds over time"
```

### Risk — Moment (Potential Quality)
What could go wrong. Risks are qualities that inhere in the decision
but have not yet manifested. Each risk MUST name itself, rate severity
(CRITICAL/HIGH/MEDIUM/LOW), and state the mitigation.

```toml
[[risks]]
name = "josh maturity (<2 years, 1 maintainer)"
severity = "HIGH"
mitigation = "Wrap in b00t-controlled proxy; vendor/josh pinned to known-good SHA; fallback to submodules if josh goes unmaintained"

[[risks]]
name = "Agent retraining surface"
severity = "MEDIUM"
mitigation = "New `just vendor-*` recipes abstract josh commands; agents never touch josh CLI directly; training datums ship with migration"
```

### Endurant — Endurant (Persistent Entity)
What persists AFTER the decision is executed. These are the concrete
artifacts, files, services, and rules that remain in the codebase
indefinitely. The Endurant section is the contract — if the migration
produces these things, the DARE is fulfilled.

```toml
[endurant]
artifacts = [
    "vendor.josh — workspace file defining all 20 vendor projections",
    "just vendor-sync — recipe to update all projections atomically",
    "just vendor-status — recipe to show projection health",
    "_b00t_/datums/josh-agent-protocol.tomllmd — agent-safe josh instructions",
    "gate: validate-vendor-workspace — CI gate checking workspace.josh parseability",
]
removed = [
    ".gitmodules (20 entries)",
    "git submodule update --init --recursive (from all CI workflows)",
    "all vendor/ gitlinks from git index",
]
```

### Lifecycle

```
Observe: 20 submodules, agent safety surface, CI pain
   ↓
Orient:  Research josh, gitbutler, gritql, git-subrepo, do-nothing
   ↓    (write Alternatives)
Decide:  Select josh + scoped adoption
   ↓    (write Decision)
   ↓    (assess Risks)
   ↓
Act:     Execute migration → produce Endurant artifacts
   ↓
Verify:  Compare actual Endurant artifacts against DARE contract
```

### DARE vs ADR

| Aspect | ADR | DARE |
|--------|-----|------|
| Focus | Record what was decided | Commit to what WILL be produced |
| Risk | Optional | Mandatory (OMO compliant) |
| UFO grounding | None | All four sections mapped to stereotypes |
| OODA integration | None | Explicit Act-phase state transition |
| Output contract | Description of decision | Enumeration of Endurant artifacts |

### DARE acceptance gate

A DARE is ACCEPTED when:
1. All four sections are present and non-empty
2. At least one Alternative is listed with a rejection reason
3. Every Risk has a mitigation
4. The Endurant section names specific files/artifacts/recipes
5. Operator has approved (captain override per PAIR-CONCEPT)

